However, despite their strong performance, these methods remain underexplored in terms of robustness, especially regarding out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization. Some studies Verwimp et al. (2021); Zhang et al. (2022b) have raised concerns that such methods may rely heavily on memorizing replayed samples rather than truly learning and generalizing the underlying knowledge. Standard (i.i.d.) generalization and overfitting in CL have themselves been examined in prior work Bonicelli et al. (2022); Yu et al. (2022); the more specific question we focus on is OOD generalization in rehearsal-based CL — that is, how well a continual learner trained on clean, ordered tasks performs under label-preserving covariate shifts at test time — which has received little systematic attention. Our investigations clearly demonstrate that current rehearsal-based approaches fail to generalize to samples exhibiting such covariate shifts (see Figure 1). Notably, by design, CL methods aim to retain knowledge from previously learned classes/tasks — in other words, to prevent forgetting. However, the critical point is that preventing forgetting should not come at the cost of memorization, which can severely degrade a model's ability to generalize, particularly to OOD data. In essence, while CL methods should preserve past knowledge, they must do so in a way that maintains the model's generalization capability not only on i.i.d. test data but also under distributional shift. This underexplored aspect — OOD generalization in rehearsal-based CL — motivates our study, where we analyze the problem and propose an effective solution.
